  "ablation_size_label": "size = 52,428,800 (and 6 benchmark sizes)",
  "takeaways": [
    "**FP64 Register Accumulation vs. Single-Precision Drift:** The official GPU MODE reference kernel (`data.to(torch.float64).sum().to(torch.float32)`) evaluates 52.4M-element reductions in double precision. Accumulating in pure FP32 caused catastrophic cancellation on randomized seeds under the strict `rtol=1e-5, atol=1e-8` threshold. Promoting register accumulators to `double` (`(to-double ...)`), running warp butterfly shuffles in 64-bit, and issuing native `atom.global.add.f64` matched the double-precision reference bit-for-bit across all public and secret seeds.",
    "**Blackwell (`B200`) Architecture Profile:** Leveraging Crisp's `def-hardware-profile b200` with 2x `float4` loads (32 bytes/thread) and 8 independent FP64 accumulators achieved **89.44 µs** public / **88.26 µs** secret (2.34 TB/s read throughput), outperforming the universal 1x float4 kernel.",
    "**Achieved Memory Bandwidth:** Reading 209.7 MB in 89.44 µs on B200 achieves **2.34 TB/s**, while H100 achieves **2.08 TB/s** (101.01 µs), A100 achieves **1.28 TB/s** (164.36 µs), and L4 achieves **215.2 GB/s** (974.52 µs).",
    "**Host Launch vs. Kernel Latency:** The remaining gap to the fastest leaderboard entries (e.g. 67.9 µs on H100) is dominated by host-side PyTorch launch overhead (`torch::zeros` allocation and `.to(kFloat32)` conversion). Future iterations using last-thread-finishes direct FP32 output will eliminate host dispatch latency."
  ]
}
